-- title:
--   Igusa branch valuation subring at a crossing end
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ and $\ell$ are primes, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`), and $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ (`hℓ12`) with $\ell \mid M'$ (`hℓM'`). Further, $L$ is a field of characteristic zero, $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hζ`), and `hι` asserts that there is a ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = e^{2\pi i/q}$. The level group $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times}$ is required (`hH₁`) to be the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times} \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^{\times}$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^{\times} \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^{\times}$. The field $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((T))$ required (`hK`) to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ over $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   The base ring $A$ is a Henselian discrete valuation ring with fraction field $L$ and algebraically closed residue field, with $q$ in its maximal ideal (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), and $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. The element $j \in K$ is required (`hj`) to have Laurent expansion [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-function, and $j \neq 0$. The element $\varpi \in A$ generates the maximal ideal (`hϖ`), and $\varpi_t \in A$ satisfies $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`).
--
--   Write $C :=$ `chartAlgFin A (↥K) j` for the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin A (↥K) j` for $j$ viewed in $C$. For a prime $q$ and an algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$, [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7) is the set of $\tilde{j} \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\tilde{j}$ has trivial $q$-torsion in its group of affine points. For $\gamma \in SL_2(\mathbb{Z})$, the predicate [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) on an $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ (below: $\tau$ is a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$) says that for every weight $k$, all modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ for the subgroup [`CohCarrier.GammaH (q ^ 2 * M') H₁`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) of $GL_2(\mathbb{R})$ having integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ with $p_g$ nonzero as a Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is $p_f/p_g$, and every embedding $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta)=e^{2\pi i/q}$, one has $\iota(\tau x)\cdot(g|_k\,\sigma)^{\wedge} = (f|_k\,\sigma)^{\wedge}$ as $q$-expansions, where $\sigma =$ `conjElemN q γ⁻¹` is the matrix obtained from $\gamma^{-1}$ by dividing the upper right entry by $q$ and multiplying the lower left entry by $q$.
--
--   The ideal $y \subseteq C$ is maximal (`hy`), contains the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), and is supersingular in the sense of `hss`: every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from $C$ to an algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ with kernel $y$ sends `jChartFin A (↥K) j` into [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7).
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` is the local rigidity input, asserted for every point $z$ of the two-chart integral model $X :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A (↥K) j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) (the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $j$-finite and $j^{-1}$-finite chart algebras) at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ (pulled back along `toBase`) lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and for every point $y'$ of `XFin A (↥K) j` $= \operatorname{Spec} C$ mapping to $z$ under `ιFin` whose ideal is supersingular in the above sense (`hss'`). Its conclusion provides a complete discrete valuation ring $W$, a homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f,u,v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f$ congruent to the Drinfeld form $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic completion of the stalk of $X$ at $z$ onto $S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v - f\,u)$, subject to seven clauses, stated in terms of the completion map $\mathrm{toC}$ from the stalk to its completion, the quotient map $\mathrm{mkS}$ onto $S$, and the homomorphism $\mathrm{germY} : C \to$ (stalk at $z$) obtained from the affine chart $\operatorname{Spec} C$ at the point $y'$: (a) $e$ carries the germ of each $a \in A$ to the class of $C(\sigma a)$; (b) every level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, preserves $C$; (c) if moreover $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$, then the induced endomorphism of $C$ is congruent to the identity modulo $y'$; (d) for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ and fixing $y'$ pointwise modulo $y'$, there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in M_2(W)$ such that $\theta$ transports $e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$ along $\tau$, fixes all constants $C(w)$, satisfies $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i C(M_{ij})X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0,X_1$, with $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}_W$, with $c \equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$, and $c \not\equiv 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; (e) two primes $P_1,P_2$ of $S$, each not containing both classes of $X_0,X_1$, each containing the class of $C(\sigma\varpi)$, and having tangent directions $(a_1,b_1)$ and $(a_2,b_2)$ in the sense that $a_iX_0+b_iX_1$ plus an element of $(X_0,X_1)^2$ lies in $P_i$, have distinct contractions to the stalk along $e \circ \mathrm{toC}$ as soon as $q \nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$; (f) for a prime $P$ of $S$ as above with tangent direction $(1,0)$, an element $a \in C$ satisfies $\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a)) \in e^{-1}P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$; (g) [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18), the $q$-fold $q$-expansion rescaling of the $j$-series, lies in $K$ and in $C$, and there are $a_0 \in A$ with this element congruent to $a_0$ modulo $y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ such that the binary form of degree $e_0$ given by the corresponding coefficients of $h$ takes unit values at all pairs $(a,b) \in W^2$ not both in $\mathfrak{m}_W$ with $a^qb-ab^q \in \mathfrak{m}_W$, and $e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\,$`jqNModC`$\,-a_0)))$ is the class of $h$.
--
--   A particular such situation is then fixed: a point $z$ of $X$, the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ (`hϖz`) lying in the maximal ideal of the stalk (`hz`), a point $y'$ of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ over $z$ (`hy'`) which is supersingular (`hss'`) and whose ideal equals $y$ (`hy'y`); together with explicit data $W_1, \sigma_1, f_1, u_1, v_1$ (with $u_1,v_1$ units, `hu₁`, `hv₁`, and $f_1$ congruent to the Drinfeld form modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, `hf₁`), $\mathfrak{m}_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$ (`hσ₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ of the completed stalk with $S_1 := W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1}))\,v_1 - f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for these data the same seven clauses (a)–(g) as above.
--
--   The ideal $J \subseteq C$ is required (`hJ`) to be the infimum of the ideals $\tau^{-1}\big((e_1 \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY})^{-1}(\,(\mathrm{mkS}(C(\sigma_1\varpi_t)), \mathrm{mkS}(X_0), \mathrm{mkS}(X_1))\,)\big)$, taken over all $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and all level automorphisms $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$. The subalgebra $B \subseteq K$ is required (`hB`) to be the $A$-algebra obtained from $C$ by adjoining all $x \in K$ with $x\varpi_t \in J$. A valuation subring $W \subseteq K$ is given with $B \subseteq W$ (`hBW`), and three groups of hypotheses on the pair $(B,W)$: `hR1` states that $C \le B$ and that every element of $K$ is a quotient of two elements of $B$ with nonzero denominator; `hR2` states that $A \to B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation and that $B/(\varpi)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; `hR3` (five clauses) states that the elements of $L$ lying in $W$ are exactly those coming from $A$, that $\mathfrak{m}_W$ is generated by $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that for $b \in C$ one has $b \in y$ if and only if $b \in \mathfrak{m}_W$, and that $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at $\mathfrak{m}_W \cap B$ in the sense that $f \in W$ if and only if $f = g/h$ with $g,h \in B$ and $h \notin \mathfrak{m}_W$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hEQ` (five clauses) describes the reduction of $B$ along $W$ and its equivariance: for every $(\mathbb{F}_{q^2})$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho$ from $B$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) whose kernel is the set of elements of $B$ lying in $\mathfrak{m}_W$, which is compatible with $A \to \mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, and which for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $W$ stable under $\tau$ intertwines $\tau$ with [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325) at an element $(\,$`redQ q γ`$,c)$ of [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of the character $(g,c)\mapsto \det(g)c^{q+1}$), with $c \neq 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$; secondly, $B$ is stable under all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; thirdly, every prime $Q$ of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is hit by some $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$, in that $\tau$ maps every element of $B$ lying in $\mathfrak{m}_W$ into $Q$; fourthly, an element of $B$ all of whose images under such $\tau$ lie in $\mathfrak{m}_W$ is divisible by $\varpi$ in $B$; fifthly, a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ on $C$ also preserves $W$.
--
--   Finally, `hjK` and `hjC` place [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) in $K$ and in $C$; $m \ge 1$ is an integer with $\varpi^m = \varpi_t w$ for some unit $w$ of $A$ (`hm1`, `hmt`); `ends` is a finite set of subrings of $K$ characterised by `hends`: a subring $O$ belongs to `ends` exactly when there are a nonzero $a \in J \subseteq C$ and a maximal ideal $P$ of the $A$-algebra $B_a$ obtained from $C$ by adjoining all $x \in K$ with $xa \in J$, such that $O$ is the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$ (i.e. $f \in O$ iff $f = g/h$ with $g,h \in B_a$, $h \notin P$), every element of $y$ that lies in $O$ is a non-unit of $O$, and $B \not\subseteq O$. One such end $O$ is fixed (`hOmem`), with $O \subseteq W$ (`hOW`), $O$ local (`hOloc`) and Noetherian (`hOnoe`), $C \subseteq O$ (`hCO`) and $\varpi \in O$ (`hϖO`). The crossing data at $O$ consist of $c_x,c_y \in O$, a unit $u$ of $O$, an isomorphism $\iota$ from the $\mathfrak{m}_O$-adic completion of $O$ onto the crossing model `UVCrossingModel (AdicCompletion (maximalIdeal A) A) (ϖ^m)`, that is $\widehat{A}[[X_0,X_1]]/(X_0X_1 - C(\varpi^m))$, and units $\gamma_U,\gamma_V$ of that model, such that $c_xc_y = \varpi^m u$ in $O$ (`hcxy`), $\iota$ matches the constants coming from $A$ (`hιc`), $\iota(c_x) = \gamma_U\,\mathrm{U}$ and $\iota(c_y) = \gamma_V\,\mathrm{V}$ for the two distinguished elements $\mathrm{U},\mathrm{V}$ of the model (`hιx`, `hιy`), while $c_y$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_W$ (`hcyW`) and $c_x$ does not (`hcxW`).
--
--   Under all these hypotheses, there exists a valuation subring $W_x$ of $K$ such that: $O \subseteq W_x$; every element of $C$ lies in $W_x$; for $x \in L$ the image of $x$ in $K$ lies in $W_x$ if and only if $x$ comes from $A$; $W_x$ is a discrete valuation ring; $\varpi$ lies in $W_x$ and generates $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$; $c_x$ lies in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$ whenever it lies in $W_x$; $c_y$ does not lie in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$ whenever it lies in $W_x$; every $b \in C$ lying in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$ lies in $y$; and there exists $b \in y$ not lying in $\mathfrak{m}_{W_x}$. Thus the roles of $c_x$ and $c_y$ are exchanged relative to $W$, and the centre of $W_x$ on the $j$-finite chart algebra is a prime strictly contained in the supersingular maximal ideal $y$.
--
--   This is the existence step for the Igusa branch attached to an end of the blow-up of the modular curve at a supersingular point in the auxiliary $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-diamond frame: from a crossing presentation $\widehat{O} \cong \widehat{A}[[U,V]]/(UV-\varpi^m)$ at an end $O$ it produces the discrete valuation of the function field $K$ along the branch on which the coordinate $c_x$ vanishes, with centre on the $j$-finite chart a proper specialisation of the supersingular point. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.exists_igusaValuationSubring_of_mem_ends_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevelOne.exists_igusaValuationSubring_of_mem_ends_blowupChart_of_drinfeldChartWitness_linked_of_dvd).
